				\begin{document}$$x_j$$\end{document} in Eq. 2 – but did not account for the evident conditional heteroskedasticity in the returns. Since that time, however, bootstrap simulation from a conditionally heteroskedastic (but serially independent) time series has become quite feasible – albeit only if the time series is symmetrically distributed; this can now be done using what is called the ‘wild’ bootstrap. Fortunately, in our application here – as is evident from the histogram for the 3M Company returns data displayed in Fig. 2, and which is similar in this regard to the histograms for all of the other stock return time series considered here – this symmetry requirement does not appear to be problematic.
A bit of background on bootstrap simulation: The ordinary bootstrap assumes that the original data are identically (as well as independently) distributed, and simply selects new – i.e., simulated – values by picking at random (with replacement) from the original sample. The wild bootstrap is different: it is aimed at reproducing in each generated time series whatever pattern of heteroskedasticity – conditional or not – existed in the original series. It does this by retaining the serial independence assumption of the ordinary bootstrap, but replacing the assumption that the data are identically distributed in each period with an assumption that the data are symmetrically distributed. The wild bootstrap then creates each ‘new’ sample observation by merely – at random – flipping the sign on this individual observation. Each new sequence of observations – i.e., each new sample – will therefore be completely different, but everything about its variance – including any pattern with which its variance evolves over time – is preserved in each of the newly generated samples. Clearly, this ‘sign flipping’ does not alter the distribution of the variate if and only if it is symmetric.
Thus, in principle, one can obtain a new sample from the wild bootstrap by just multiplying the original error term in the model (independently in each period) by any zero-mean, unit-variance variate that strikes one’s fancy. Most people, however, simply use for this purpose a simple Bernoulli random variable, that takes on the values of either plus or minus one.
Asymptotically, this distributional choice in the application of the wild bootstrap doesn’t matter. But in finite samples, it turns out that it does – especially in somewhat-fraught cases such as ours, where one has a toxic brew of both fairly strong conditional heteroskedasticity and (for a number of stocks) at least some degree of serial correlation. Fortunately for us, that is precisely the case which was extensively investigated by Gonçalves and Kilian (6), whose extensive simulation work indicates that using realizations of an independently distributed unit normal to flip the signs works well in this setting; so that is the version of the wild bootstrap used here.
