Indeed, we could really just as well label this column of Table 2 with the heading ‘Frequency’ as ‘P-value’. Before going on to those results, however, we think that it is worthwhile consider this point a bit more deeply.
A sensible way to interpret a rejection P-value is to posit that one either does – or does not – reject the null hypothesis, based upon the sample evidence against it. Then, upon repeated sampling – in a frequentist world, where one imagines that such repeated sampling is a possibility – and (notably) where the null hypothesis is actually true, the rejection P-value is just the fraction of the repeated samples where one (incorrectly) rejects the null hypothesis. This, as we have hopefully made clear above, is precisely what we are actually calculating with our bootstrap simulations, and quoting as the empirical P-value (or ‘frequency’) in this column of Table 2.7
A ‘frequency’ or P-value close to zero means that there is overwhelmingly strong sample evidence against the null hypothesis: the distribution of simulated maximum absolute excursions away from the trend line under the serial independence (random walk) assumption exhibits simulated returns wandering off away from the trend line to a greater extent across the 10,000 simulations than we observe in the actual 3M Company data. Whereas, a ‘frequency’ approaching one half means that there is no empirical evidence whatever against the null hypothesis: the distribution of simulated maximum absolute excursions away from the trend line under the serial independence (random walk) assumption has simulated returns wandering off away from the trend line about as much as one might expect (in its median value) as is observed in the actual data 3M Company data.
Note that, from this point of view, there is clearly nothing special about a P-value of 0.05. Indeed – from this perspective – it was fundamentally a bit misleading to discuss the simulation-based calculation discussed above in terms of a ‘hypothesis test’ – with a ‘rejection P-value’ – in the first place: More meaningfully, what we have actually done here, with the pre-whitened 3M Company daily returns data, is that we have simulated 10, 000 new returns time series – based on the assumption that these returns are serially independent – and found that the serial independence assumption yields too much dispersion away from the trend in about 94%\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
