From this perspective, then, we invite the reader to reframe the results of the AP ‘test’ in these terms, and to view the figures in the ‘P-value’ column of Table 2 as “the estimated probability that data simulated data under the hypothesis that returns are serially independent, and hence will deviate from the trend line even more than the amount observed” – rather than only as “the simulation-based rejection P-value for null hypothesis that returns are serially independent” – even though these two statements are actually equivalent.8
Furthermore: Intuitively – we interpret a small sample value of the P-value – i.e., a value close to zero – as empirical evidence that the returns for this stock show signs of having been materially affected by some sort of ‘restoring force’ in its market – after having wandered (perhaps repeatedly) too far away from the trend during the course of the sample period. Whereas, we would interpret a large sample value of the P-value – i.e., a value close to one-half – as an absence of empirical evidence for any sign of materially significant impacts of such a ‘restoring force’ on the returns for stock in this firm. Perhaps this absence of evidence is because the market in which this particular firm trades is such that such restoring forces are very weak. Or perhaps this absence is because the returns for the stock in this firm never (or too rarely) wandered sufficiently far away from the trend to be affected such market forces.
In contrast, while it certainly is possible to observe a value for the ‘frequency’ or P-value in the interval [0.50, 1.0], it is not so clear what economic interpretation to place on such an observation, although physicists seem to have no compunctions about inventing bizarre repellent forces in like circumstances. A result of this nature would seem to us to arise instead from either sampling errors, the use of a sample which is seriously too small for this task, and/or fairly severe model misspecification issues.9 Fortunately, reference to Table 2 indicates that empirical P-value observations in this difficult-to-interpret range are uncommon.10This concludes our description of the AP test, as updated here, via an application to the new sample data on daily returns to the 3M Company. In Sect. 3 below we next describe the rest of the new sample data set assembled for the present study. There we both report on and interpret the results of applying the updated AP test to these new data.

