Nevertheless – as in Ashley and Patterson (1) – we have evidently obtained some notable evidence against the random walk null hypothesis here: to the point where our statement above that ‘we have obtained some ‘not insubstantial’ – albeit modest – evidence against the null hypothesis’ seems a like a reasonable assertion for us to make. Perhaps that is the best that can be really said at the moment.

Second – and perhaps this is partially because we are here looking at 15 rejection P-value results in Table 2, rather than the just the 5 P-value results in Table 1 of Ashley and Patterson (1) – we are now inclined to view these P-values a bit more broadly than as just the frequency with which one would incorrectly reject the (serial iid) null hypothesis (upon repeated sampling), were it actually valid. In particular, we now think it useful to take this P-value, for a particular firm’s stock returns, as an empirical measure of the degree to which there is a some sort of – likely nonlinear – ‘restoring force’ – omitted from the geometric quasi-random walk model, Eq. (1) – systematically tending to return ln(Pt)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
